/// A network interface card, **link layer only** (docs/driver-plan.md §3.8,
/// docs/networking-plan.md §5). No IP, ARP or DHCP: those belong to the stack
/// service `netd`, which is just another client of this interface and, by
/// policy, the only one. A NIC driver (`netdrv`, virtio-net first) serves it and
/// never parses a payload.
///
/// **The client owns the rings.** Replies cannot carry buffers or handles (the
/// kernel refuses transfers in a reply), and the driver must not let its device
/// read memory a client can rewrite, so the client creates two shared buffers
/// and the driver copies frames between them and its own DMA slots, in both
/// directions (the audio rule, see `os.lazy.audio.v1`). A ring is the
/// single-producer/single-consumer frame ring of `libs/framering`: fixed
/// 2048-byte slots, a `u16` length then the frame, a power-of-two slot count,
/// free-running `u32` indices in a header page. Nothing about the wire lives in
/// a request body except the slot count.
///
/// `AttachRing` carries, in the parcel's `handles` and `buffers` vectors (the
/// TLV body has no `Handle`/`Buffer` fields, as in `os.lazy.display.v1`):
///
/// * `buffers[0]`: one shared buffer holding **both rings**, back to back: the
///   **receive ring** (driver produces, client consumes) at byte 0 and the
///   **transmit ring** (client produces, driver consumes) at byte
///   `framering::ring_bytes(slots)`. (The kernel surfaces only the first
///   transferred buffer of a request to its receiver, so two rings cannot
///   travel as two buffers.)
/// * `handles[0]`: the **notify endpoint**, an endpoint the client holds the
///   receiving side of, to which the driver posts `Notify`.
///
/// The buffer must be exactly `2 * framering::ring_bytes(slots)` bytes (two
/// header pages plus `2 * slots` slots) or the call fails with `EINVAL`;
/// `slots` must be a power of two from 16 to 1024 or it fails with `EINVAL`. Only one client may
/// be attached (`EBUSY` otherwise); its owner is the kernel-stamped sender of
/// `AttachRing`, and calls on the ring by anyone else fail with `EACCES`.
///
/// **Wake-up.** This interface replaces the draft's `notify: String` topic: a
/// topic would put a broker round trip on the receive path. Instead each
/// direction has one one-way message and one shared flag, so a waiter needs a
/// single wait on a single endpoint and a burst costs one message, not one per
/// frame (the rule the kernel uses for interrupt messages):
///
/// * driver to client, `Notify`: posted to the notify endpoint when frames
///   arrive in the receive ring, when the transmit ring gains space after
///   being full, or when the link changes;
/// * client to driver, `Kick`: sent to the driver's endpoint after the client
///   queues frames in the transmit ring.
///
/// A message is sent only when the consumer of that ring has *armed* it: the
/// consumer sets the ring's `armed` flag before it sleeps (after a final look at
/// the ring, so a frame that lands in between is never missed) and the producer
/// clears it with one atomic exchange when it sends. That coalesces a burst to
/// at most one outstanding message per ring, with no kernel help. A consumer
/// that never arms simply polls; it can only harm itself.
///
/// Both sides treat the other as hostile: every index and length read from
/// shared memory is validated and read exactly once, and a frame is copied out
/// before it is looked at. A ring whose peer breaks the protocol (an index
/// that claims more frames than the ring holds, an oversized length) is
/// poisoned: the driver counts it in `NicStats.ring_errors` and detaches the
/// client. A frame shorter than the 14-byte Ethernet header or longer than
/// `NicInfo.max_frame` is dropped and counted, never truncated.
///
/// Failures of calls are returned as the shared structured error field
/// (`services::error_field`) instead of the declared reply fields.
interface os.lazy.net.nic.v1 {
    /// Describe the card: MAC, MTU, link state and the negotiated feature set.
    method Info() -> (info: NicInfo);
    /// Choose which received frames reach the client: an `RxMode` ordinal.
    /// The reply says whether the driver applied it (a device without a
    /// filter table treats `Filtered` as `Promiscuous` and answers `false`).
    /// Only the attached client may change it.
    method SetRxMode(mode: U32) -> (ok: Bool);
    /// Attach the client's rings and notify endpoint (see the interface
    /// notes for what the parcel carries). `slots` is the slot count of both
    /// rings. The reply is the ring id later calls name.
    method AttachRing(slots: U32) -> (ring: U32);
    /// Release the rings; the driver stops reading and writing them. Also
    /// implied when the owner exits or its notify endpoint reports the peer
    /// gone.
    method DetachRing(ring: U32) -> ();
    /// Frame and error counters since the driver started.
    method Stats() -> (stats: NicStats);
    /// Event, client to driver: the client queued frames in the transmit ring
    /// of `ring`. Ignored from anyone but the owner.
    method Kick(ring: U32) -> () oneway;
    /// Event, driver to client: something needs the client's attention.
    /// `events` is a bitmap: bit *n* is set for the `NotifyBit` with ordinal
    /// *n*. Reserved bits are zero; a client ignores them.
    method Notify(ring: U32, events: U32) -> () oneway;

    /// Card description.
    struct NicInfo {
        /// Six octets, network order.
        mac: Bytes,
        /// Payload bytes of the largest frame the card carries.
        mtu: U32,
        /// Largest complete frame (`mtu` plus the 14-byte Ethernet header),
        /// the bound the driver enforces in both directions.
        max_frame: U32,
        link: Bool,
        /// Capability bitmap, zero if none: bit 0 receive checksum offload,
        /// bit 1 transmit checksum offload, bit 2 VLAN tag insert/strip. Other
        /// bits are reserved: a driver sets them to zero and a client ignores
        /// them.
        features: U32
    }

    /// Counters since the driver started.
    struct NicStats {
        rx_frames: U64,
        tx_frames: U64,
        rx_bytes: U64,
        tx_bytes: U64,
        /// Frames dropped on receive for any reason (no ring, ring full, and
        /// the invalid frames below).
        rx_dropped: U64,
        /// Frames dropped on transmit for any reason (queue full, and the
        /// invalid frames below).
        tx_dropped: U64,
        /// Frames shorter than the 14-byte Ethernet header, either direction.
        runts: U64,
        /// Frames longer than `max_frame`, either direction.
        oversize: U64,
        /// Rings poisoned by a peer that broke the protocol, plus device
        /// used-ring entries the driver rejected.
        ring_errors: U64,
        /// Interrupt messages the driver handled (0 when polling).
        interrupts: U64,
        link_changes: U32
    }

    /// The payload of `system/net/{nic}/link`.
    struct LinkEvent {
        up: Bool,
        /// Link changes since the driver started, so a subscriber can tell a
        /// flap from a repeat.
        changes: U32
    }

    /// Published by the driver whenever the link changes, and once at start.
    /// `{nic}` is the driver's card name (`virtio-net0`).
    topic "system/net/{nic}/link" : LinkEvent retained qos=latest;

    /// Reasons for a `Notify`; ordinals are the `events` bit numbers, so new
    /// ones are append-only. `RxReady`: frames arrived in the receive ring.
    /// `TxSpace`: the transmit ring gained space after being full.
    /// `LinkChange`: the link went up or down.
    enum NotifyBit { RxReady, TxSpace, LinkChange }

    /// Which received frames reach the client.
    enum RxMode { Off, Filtered, Promiscuous }
}

/// The network stack service `netd` (docs/networking-plan.md N2): address
/// configuration, routes, statistics and ping, over the one interface the
/// stack drives (`os.lazy.net.nic.v1`). `netd` runs as the `_netd` user with no
/// capabilities, is the only client of the NIC driver, and parses every frame
/// the network sends it: nothing here is authority over a device.
///
/// **Nothing names a caller.** Who is asking is the kernel-stamped sender of the
/// call; `Renew` and other changes are gated by the ACL (`libs/netpolicy`), not
/// by a field in a request. Failures are returned as the shared structured error
/// field (`services::error_field`) instead of the declared reply fields.
///
/// **Blocking without threads.** `Ping` is a synchronous call whose reply is
/// *parked*: `netd` answers it when the echo reply arrives, or with `ETIMEDOUT`
/// when `timeout_ms` passes, and the caller sleeps in the kernel meanwhile with
/// its own deadline (`msg_cancel` and the caller's death clean up). One
/// caller may have up to 4 pings outstanding and all callers together 8; past
/// either limit the call fails with `EAGAIN`. The stack's clock is the 100 Hz tick, so round trips read in
/// multiples of 10 ms.
interface os.lazy.net.stack.v1 {
    /// The interfaces the stack drives (one today).
    method Interfaces() -> (list: Array<InterfaceInfo>);
    /// The addresses configured on every interface.
    method Addresses() -> (list: Array<AddressInfo>);
    /// The routing table.
    method Routes() -> (list: Array<RouteInfo>);
    /// Stack counters since it started.
    method Stats() -> (stats: StackStats);
    /// Send one ICMP echo request to `dst` (four octets) with `payload_len`
    /// bytes of payload (0 to 1400) and wait for its reply. `timeout_ms`
    /// (10 to 60000) bounds the wait. Fails with `EINVAL` for a bad argument,
    /// `ENETUNREACH` when there is no address or route yet, `EAGAIN` when this
    /// caller has 4 pings outstanding or all callers together have 8, and `ETIMEDOUT` when no reply came.
    method Ping(dst: Bytes, payload_len: U32, timeout_ms: U32) -> (result: EchoResult);
    /// Ask for a fresh DHCP lease (drops the current one). Gated by the ACL.
    method Renew() -> ();
    /// Drop the attachment to the NIC driver and establish it again, as after
    /// a driver restart: the stack keeps its lease and timers. A recovery and
    /// test call; gated by the ACL.
    method Reattach() -> ();

    /// One network interface.
    struct InterfaceInfo {
        /// The interface name (`eth0`).
        name: String,
        /// Six octets, network order.
        mac: Bytes,
        mtu: U32,
        /// The driver reports the link up.
        link: Bool,
        /// A `ConfigMode` ordinal: how the interface is configured.
        mode: U32,
        /// A `DhcpState` ordinal (`Off` for a static interface).
        dhcp: U32
    }

    /// One configured IPv4 address.
    struct AddressInfo {
        interface: String,
        /// Four octets, network order.
        addr: Bytes,
        prefix_len: U32,
        /// An `AddrSource` ordinal.
        source: U32,
        /// Seconds of lease left when asked (0 when static or unknown).
        lease_secs: U32
    }

    /// One IPv4 route.
    struct RouteInfo {
        interface: String,
        /// Four octets; all zero with `prefix_len` 0 is the default route.
        dest: Bytes,
        prefix_len: U32,
        /// Four octets; all zero for an on-link route.
        gateway: Bytes
    }

    /// Stack counters since it started.
    struct StackStats {
        /// Frames read from and written to the NIC rings.
        rx_frames: U64,
        tx_frames: U64,
        rx_bytes: U64,
        tx_bytes: U64,
        /// Frames the stack wanted to send but the transmit ring had no room for.
        tx_dropped: U64,
        /// Frames the driver's ring delivered with an impossible length.
        rx_bad_length: U64,
        /// Times the NIC ring or the driver went away and was re-attached.
        nic_resets: U64,
        /// DHCP leases acquired and lost.
        leases: U64,
        lease_losses: U64,
        /// Pings started, answered, and timed out.
        pings_sent: U64,
        pings_answered: U64,
        pings_timed_out: U64
    }

    /// The answer to one echo request.
    struct EchoResult {
        /// Round trip in milliseconds (10 ms resolution).
        rtt_ms: U32,
        /// Four octets: who answered.
        source: Bytes,
        /// Bytes of echo payload returned.
        bytes: U32
    }

    /// The address `netd` currently holds on an interface, retained so a late
    /// subscriber sees it. `{ifname}` is the interface name.
    topic "system/net/{ifname}/addr" : AddressEvent retained qos=latest;

    /// Published once when the stack first has an address (and again after a
    /// lease is lost and regained).
    topic "system/events/network/up" : AddressEvent qos=latest;

    /// An interface's address state.
    struct AddressEvent {
        interface: String,
        /// Four octets; all zero when there is no address.
        addr: Bytes,
        prefix_len: U32,
        /// Four octets; all zero when there is no gateway.
        gateway: Bytes
    }

    /// How an interface is configured.
    enum ConfigMode { Dhcp, Static }

    /// Where an address came from.
    enum AddrSource { Dhcp, Static }

    /// The DHCP client's state as the stack can observe it: a lease is held
    /// (`Bound`) or it is being looked for (`Discovering`, which includes asking
    /// the server to confirm an offer).
    enum DhcpState { Off, Discovering, Bound }
}
